<body>
    <h1>AI-266: Migrating from Euclidean Distance to Cosine Similarity</h1>
    <div class=3D"Section1">
        <p>We needed to change the facial recognition workflow with a funda=
mental change: switch from using Euclidean distance to cosine similarity fo=
r comparing facial embeddings. This change was driven by the need to improv=
e the accuracy and robustness of the system, particularly in dealing with h=
igh-dimensional data and normalized embeddings.</p>
<h1 id=3D"AI-266:MigratingfromEuclideanDistancetoCosineSimilarity-OriginalI=
mplementation">Original Implementation</h1>
<p>The initial face recognition system followed a standard approach:</p>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p><strong>Face Detection:</strong> Faces were detected in images using a f=
ace detection algorithm (e.g., one provided by DeepFace or OpenCV).</p></li=
>
<li>
<p><strong>Embedding Generation:</strong> A pre-trained face recognition mo=
del (e.g., FaceNet512, ArcFace) was used to generate a numerical representa=
tion (embedding) of each detected face. This embedding captured the key fac=
ial features in a high-dimensional vector.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Database Storage:</strong> The facial embeddings were stored in =
a vector database (ChromaDB), along with associated metadata (e.g., name, I=
D, image source). The database was configured to use Euclidean distance (L2=
 distance) as the metric for comparing vectors.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Recognition:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Given a query image, the face was detected, and its embedding was genera=
ted.</p></li>
<li>
<p>The system queried the database to find the embedding that was closest t=
o the query embedding, based on Euclidean distance.</p></li>
<li>
<p>If the Euclidean distance between the query embedding and the closest da=
tabase embedding was below a certain threshold, the system declared a match=
 and identified the face.</p></li>
</ul></li>
</ol>
<p>Euclidean distance was chosen initially because:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>It is a simple and widely used distance metric.</p></li>
<li>
<p>It is readily available in most vector databases (including ChromaDB).</=
p></li>
</ul>
<p>Despite the initial rationale, several problems were observed with the E=
uclidean distance-based approach:</p>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p><strong>Unintuitive Distance Values:</strong> The raw Euclidean distance=
 values were difficult to interpret as a measure of similarity. The range o=
f possible distance values depended on the characteristics of the embedding=
 space and the magnitude of the vector components, making it challenging to=
 set an appropriate threshold.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Sensitivity to Vector Magnitude:</strong> Euclidean distance is =
sensitive to the magnitude (length) of the vectors. This meant that two vec=
tors pointing in roughly the same direction (i.e., representing similar fac=
es) could have a large Euclidean distance if one vector had a significantly=
 larger magnitude than the other.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Inconsistent Results:</strong> The system produced inconsistent =
and unreliable recognition results, particularly with faces that were not p=
erfectly aligned or had variations in lighting or expression.</p></li>
</ol>
<p>As an example, the core of our problem was that a higher distance meant =
different things under different situations:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>High distance =3D similar: if a face looks similar when the l2 norm of a=
ll other is 0, then these small faces will have high distances to them!</p>=
</li>
<li>
<p>Low distance =3D Dissimilar: if a face looks dissimilar with 1e99 pixels=
, then the vectors will be extremely large!</p></li>
</ul>
<p>It also made it hard to set a threshold - should the high faces count or=
 not? Since a model can only operate on a certain subset, then there has to=
 be a more robust and consistent signal in order to make these determinatio=
ns.</p>
<h1 id=3D"AI-266:MigratingfromEuclideanDistancetoCosineSimilarity-CosineSim=
ilarity">Cosine Similarity</h1>
<p>After careful analysis, it was decided to switch to using cosine similar=
ity for comparing facial embeddings.</p>
<p>Cosine similarity offered several advantages over Euclidean distance for=
 this application:</p>
<p><strong>Directly Measures Angular Similarity:</strong> Cosine similarity=
 measures the angle between two vectors, which is a more intuitive and robu=
st measure of similarity than Euclidean distance, especially in high-dimens=
ional spaces. The angle is a better indicator of whether two vectors are po=
inting in the same direction, regardless of their magnitudes.</p>
<p><strong>Inherent Normalization:</strong> Cosine similarity is inherently=
 normalized for vector magnitude. This means that the similarity score is n=
ot affected by the length of the vectors, only their direction. This is par=
ticularly important when working with face embeddings, as the magnitude of =
the embedding vector typically doesn't have any meaningful interpretation i=
n terms of facial identity.</p>
<p><strong>Better Performance in High Dimensions:</strong> In high-dimensio=
nal spaces, Euclidean distance can become less effective as a measure of si=
milarity due to the "curse of dimensionality." As the number of dimensions =
increases, the distances between vectors tend to become more uniform, makin=
g it harder to distinguish between similar and dissimilar vectors. Cosine s=
imilarity is less susceptible to this problem.</p>
<p><strong>Natural Range:</strong> Cosine similarity is naturally bounded b=
etween -1 and 1, where:</p>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p>1 indicates perfect similarity (vectors pointing in the same direction).=
</p></li>
<li>
<p>0 indicates orthogonality (vectors are unrelated).</p></li>
<li>
<p>-1 indicates perfect dissimilarity (vectors pointing in opposite directi=
ons).</p></li>
</ol>
<p>This natural range makes it easier to set meaningful thresholds and inte=
rpret the similarity scores.</p>
<p>The switch to cosine similarity involved the following steps:</p>
<p><strong>Embedding Normalization:</strong> It was ensured that all facial=
 embeddings were L2 normalized to unit length <em>before</em> storing them =
in the database. This is a crucial step because cosine similarity is most e=
ffective when used with normalized vectors. L2 normalization involves divid=
ing each element of the vector by its L2 norm (Euclidean norm), resulting i=
n a vector with a magnitude of 1.</p>
<p><strong>ChromaDB Configuration:</strong> The ChromaDB collection was rec=
onfigured to use cosine similarity as the distance metric. This involved se=
tting the "hnsw:space" metadata parameter to "cosine" during collection cre=
ation or update.</p>
<p><strong>Similarity Calculation:</strong> The code for calculating simila=
rity scores was updated to use cosine similarity instead of Euclidean dista=
nce. With normalized vectors, cosine similarity can be efficiently computed=
 using a dot product:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">      c=
osine_similarity =3D np.dot(embedding1, embedding2)
    </pre>
</div>
</div>
<p><strong>Threshold Adjustment:</strong> The similarity threshold used for=
 face recognition was adjusted to reflect the new range of cosine similarit=
y values (i.e., -1 to 1).</p>
<p>Okay, let's add a section explaining HNSW and detailing the configuratio=
n choices made for ourr face recognition system:</p>
<h1 id=3D"AI-266:MigratingfromEuclideanDistancetoCosineSimilarity-ChromaDB"=
>ChromaDB</h1>
<p>It=E2=80=99s important to understand, in detail, exactly WHAT a vector d=
atabase does under the hood. At its core, ChromaDB uses an algorithm called=
 Hierarchical Navigable Small World (HNSW) internally to measure distances =
between vectors. HNSW implements efficient nearest neighbor search. The mai=
n problem for searching "close vectors" in high dimensionality is that it's=
 a <em>huge</em> processing workload that can lead to massive slowdowns and=
 even crashes.</p>
<p>It builds a multi-layer graph structure, where each layer is a "small wo=
rld" graph. In a small world graph, most nodes are not direct neighbors of =
each other, but most nodes can be reached from every other by a small numbe=
r of hops or steps.</p>
<p>This is an "approximate" method. The goal is <em>not</em> to find the ab=
solute nearest neighbors with 100% certainty (which would be too slow). Ins=
tead, it aims to find neighbors that are "close enough" with high probabili=
ty, and it does so very quickly. For any dataset over a few hundred data sa=
mples, HNSW is usually the best candidate for indexing and running high-dim=
ensional data.</p>
<p>Some advantages</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Speed:</strong> HNSW provides fast nearest-neighbor search, espe=
cially in high-dimensional spaces.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Scalability:</strong> It can efficiently handle large datasets.<=
/p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Accuracy vs. Speed Trade-off:</strong> HNSW allows us to tune th=
e trade-off between search accuracy and speed by adjusting its configuratio=
n parameters.</p></li>
</ul>
<h3 id=3D"AI-266:MigratingfromEuclideanDistancetoCosineSimilarity-HNSWConfi=
gurationParameters">HNSW Configuration Parameters</h3>
<p>The behavior of HNSW is controlled by several configuration parameters:<=
/p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>space:</strong> This parameter specifies the distance metric use=
d to measure the distance between vectors. It determines how the HNSW graph=
 is built and how similarity searches are performed. In our system, we are =
using "cosine" to support the properties of a unit vector as detailed in th=
e last section. The alternative is L2, which requires more steps.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>M (Maximum Connections per Layer):</strong> This parameter contr=
ols the maximum number of connections (neighbors) that each node can have i=
n each layer of the graph.</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Higher M values create a denser graph, which can improve search accuracy=
 but also increase memory usage and indexing time.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Lower M values create a sparser graph, which reduces memory usage and in=
dexing time but can potentially decrease search accuracy.</p></li>
<li>
<p>A typical range for M is 16-64. Our implementation uses 16 set as a defa=
ult.</p></li>
</ul></li>
<li>
<p><strong>ef_construction (Construction Effort):</strong> This parameter c=
ontrols the thoroughness of the index building process. It determines how m=
uch time the algorithm spends exploring the graph structure during index cr=
eation.</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Higher ef_construction values result in a more accurate index but also i=
ncrease indexing time.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Lower ef_construction values reduce indexing time but can potentially de=
crease search accuracy.</p></li>
<li>
<p>A higher ef_construction often comes with a higher search, so to better =
manage accuracy, there must be a reasonable threshold.</p></li>
</ul></li>
<li>
<p><strong>ef_search (Search Effort):</strong> This parameter controls how =
thoroughly the index is searched during query time. It determines how many =
potential neighbors the algorithm explores during the search process.</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Higher ef_search values result in more accurate search results but also =
increase query latency (search time).</p></li>
<li>
<p>Lower ef_search values reduce query latency but can potentially decrease=
 search accuracy.</p></li>
</ul></li>
</ul>
<p>For our face recognition system, we have chosen the following HNSW confi=
guration parameters, which are shown in the code:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>"hnsw:space": "cosine": As discussed in detail earlier, we are using cos=
ine similarity as the distance metric.</p></li>
<li>
<p>"hnsw:ef_construction": Because we're assuming we're adding fewer nodes =
to the training dataset and that accuracy is key, then it must have a large=
r value.</p></li>
<li>
<p>"hnsw:search_ef": This should be a reasonable number, from 50-100, based=
 on how the data is expected.</p></li>
</ul>
<p>These values represent a trade-off between accuracy and performance.</p>
<p></p>
    </div>
</body>
